# R41 T-FMT-A §4 — the THIRD shape of the paren parse-order class, and the
# only one that breaks a WORKING program. Found while enumerating `Expr` for
# the leftmost-sigil predicate: a MOVE CLOSURE also emits a leading `^`.
#
# AXIS CELL: `parse_call_arg` + `Expr::Closure { is_move: true }`. The parens
# make the `^` bind to the CLOSURE (`ConsumeCallable`, what `apply_once` wants)
# rather than to the argument.
#
# INTENDED: runs and prints `10`, before AND after `gg fmt`. Pre-fix, fmt
# emitted `apply_once(^(int x): x + n, 3)` — the move-closure became a plain
# closure moved as an argument, and the program STOPPED COMPILING
# (E_OwnershipMismatch: "the parameter only borrows"). A green-on-both-sides
# reject-to-reject cell would have missed this; this cell is the reason the
# differential helper compares RUN OUTPUT, not just the accept/reject bit.

int apply_once(ConsumeCallable[int(int)] f, int x):
    return f(x)

void main():
    int n = 7
    print(apply_once((^(int x): x + n), 3))
